1
00:00:04,430 --> 00:00:06,060
Ma, Ma, calm down.

2
00:00:06,060 --> 00:00:07,010
Listen to me.

3
00:00:07,010 --> 00:00:08,210
I know it says click with the mouse,

4
00:00:08,210 --> 00:00:11,160
but on a laptop, the trackpad is the mouse.

5
00:00:11,750 --> 00:00:13,660
Now, put your finger on it.

6
00:00:14,310 --> 00:00:16,640
Doesn't matter which finger.

7
00:00:17,940 --> 00:00:19,510
Good choice.

8
00:00:20,140 --> 00:00:22,680
Now move it down to your e-mail icon.

9
00:00:22,680 --> 00:00:25,040
Yeah, the little envelope.

10
00:00:25,680 --> 00:00:27,090
What do you mean, "What does it look like?"

11
00:00:27,090 --> 00:00:29,120
It looks like an envelope!

12
00:00:29,390 --> 00:00:30,600
Fine, you don't like the computer,

13
00:00:30,600 --> 00:00:31,870
don't use it!

14
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:36,190
Sure, we can exchange it for a salad spinner. Good-bye!

15
00:00:38,950 --> 00:00:41,690
That's a Hanukkah present you're regretting, huh?

16
00:00:42,510 --> 00:00:45,290
I won't say that all senior citizens

17
00:00:45,290 --> 00:00:48,740
who can't master technology should be publicly flogged,

18
00:00:48,740 --> 00:00:50,810
but if we made an example of one or two,

19
00:00:50,810 --> 00:00:53,600
it might give the others incentive to try harder.

20
00:00:55,190 --> 00:00:57,020
I had a great idea.

21
00:00:57,020 --> 00:00:58,070
You know how we're always having

22
00:00:58,070 --> 00:01:00,400
to stop and solve differential equations,

23
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:02,070
like when you're doing Fourier analysis,

24
00:01:02,070 --> 00:01:04,050
or using the Schr dinger equation?

25
00:01:04,050 --> 00:01:06,890
Howard doesn't. He's only an engineer.

26
00:01:07,900 --> 00:01:10,930
I was thinking, we could write a little app

27
00:01:10,930 --> 00:01:12,490
that would use handwriting recognition,

28
00:01:12,490 --> 00:01:14,840
and then run it through a symbolic evaluation engine.

29
00:01:14,840 --> 00:01:16,310
You just use your smartphone,

30
00:01:16,310 --> 00:01:18,620
take a picture of the equation and bam!

31
00:01:18,620 --> 00:01:22,780
You know what's a great app? The one that makes fart noises.

32
00:01:27,620 --> 00:01:29,220
You know, Leonard,

33
00:01:29,220 --> 00:01:31,200
that's actually a valid idea.

34
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:33,030
Very good.

35
00:01:33,450 --> 00:01:35,990
Can you say that and not make it sound like I'm a cat

36
00:01:35,990 --> 00:01:38,330
who learned how to use the toilet?

37
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:40,720
No.

38
00:01:40,720 --> 00:01:42,600
The two achievements are equally surprising

39
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:43,950
and equally admirable,

40
00:01:43,950 --> 00:01:44,970
though if pressed,

41
00:01:44,970 --> 00:01:47,640
I'd have to give a slight edge to the cat.

42
00:01:47,640 --> 00:01:49,670
I'll save you the pain and nervousness

43
00:01:49,670 --> 00:01:51,400
of waiting for the answer.

44
00:01:51,400 --> 00:01:54,860
I agree to be part of your project. Congratulations.

45
00:01:55,390 --> 00:01:57,360
Oh, yay for me.

46
00:01:58,590 --> 00:01:59,370
What about you guys?

47
00:01:59,370 --> 00:02:00,450
I can't promise anything,

48
00:02:00,450 --> 00:02:02,920
but people do make money off stuff like this.

49
00:02:02,920 --> 00:02:04,830
A few extra bucks would be nice.

50
00:02:04,830 --> 00:02:06,980
I could finally move out of my mother's house.

51
00:02:06,980 --> 00:02:07,860
Where would you go?

52
00:02:07,860 --> 00:02:08,760
I always dreamed about

53
00:02:08,760 --> 00:02:11,970
building a little place of my own over the garage

54
00:02:13,120 --> 00:02:15,400
You know, if I made more money, I could take time off from work

55
00:02:15,400 --> 00:02:18,090
and give pretty girls submarine rides.

56
00:02:18,090 --> 00:02:18,820
What's that?

57
00:02:18,820 --> 00:02:20,950
Some weird sex thing?

58
00:02:22,360 --> 00:02:23,130
No.

59
00:02:23,130 --> 00:02:24,380
You take pretty girls underwater

60
00:02:24,380 --> 00:02:27,150
in your private submarine, and you show them fish.

61
00:02:27,740 --> 00:02:30,170
Why does everything have to be dirty with you?

62
00:02:30,820 --> 00:02:33,160
I was thinking we could work on this at night,

63
00:02:33,160 --> 00:02:34,660
and then maybe in a couple of weeks,

64
00:02:34,660 --> 00:02:36,510
we'll have ourselves an app to sell.

65
00:02:36,510 --> 00:02:38,020
Sounds like we're in business.

66
00:02:38,020 --> 00:02:40,120
I think we should take a picture to capture this moment.

67
00:02:40,120 --> 00:02:41,800
- Yeah. - Oh!

68
00:02:47,240 --> 00:02:50,250
Was that the best 99 cents I ever spent, or what?

69
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:17,130
I checked the App Store.

70
00:03:17,130 --> 00:03:18,050
No one is doing

71
00:03:18,050 --> 00:03:20,640
handwriting-recognition differential equation solving.

72
00:03:20,640 --> 00:03:22,980
Oh, dear Lord, shush.

73
00:03:24,290 --> 00:03:25,010
What?

74
00:03:25,010 --> 00:03:27,690
You have so few good ideas, Leonard.

75
00:03:27,690 --> 00:03:28,980
And you're just going to spill the beans

76
00:03:28,980 --> 00:03:31,350
in front of an outsider?

77
00:03:38,060 --> 00:03:39,390
Who, me?

78
00:03:39,690 --> 00:03:41,530
Sheldon, I don't think Penny cares

79
00:03:41,530 --> 00:03:43,820
about handwriting-recognition based differential...

80
00:03:43,820 --> 00:03:46,470
*The stars at night are big and bright*

81
00:03:46,470 --> 00:03:48,920
*Deep in the heart of Texas*

82
00:03:49,430 --> 00:03:51,540
She's not going to steal our idea.

83
00:03:51,540 --> 00:03:53,110
- What idea? - We're gonna write an application...

84
00:03:53,110 --> 00:03:55,810
*The prairie sky is wide and high*

85
00:03:55,810 --> 00:03:56,850
*Deep in the heart...*

86
00:03:56,850 --> 00:03:58,450
Okay, stop, stop!

87
00:03:58,450 --> 00:03:59,970
*Of Texas*

88
00:04:00,890 --> 00:04:01,900
Whatever your secret is,

89
00:04:01,900 --> 00:04:04,110
I'm sure it's boring, so I'm not interested.

90
00:04:04,110 --> 00:04:06,310
It's not boring at all.

91
00:04:06,310 --> 00:04:07,620
You see, in higher order mathematics,

92
00:04:07,620 --> 00:04:08,470
there are certain sets

93
00:04:08,470 --> 00:04:10,450
- of equations that... - Bored.

94
00:04:11,280 --> 00:04:12,240
Satisfied?

95
00:04:12,240 --> 00:04:13,110
Hardly.

96
00:04:13,110 --> 00:04:16,190
Consider this unlikely but very plausible scenario.

97
00:04:16,190 --> 00:04:19,160
A young woman alone in the big city.

98
00:04:19,160 --> 00:04:20,390
Her ridiculous dream

99
00:04:20,390 --> 00:04:23,240
of becoming an actress lies shattered about her.

100
00:04:23,670 --> 00:04:24,850
Hey, wait a minute.

101
00:04:24,850 --> 00:04:26,560
Well, hang on. Let's see where he's going.

102
00:04:27,160 --> 00:04:28,300
Then it hits her.

103
00:04:28,300 --> 00:04:29,990
How is she going to survive?

104
00:04:29,990 --> 00:04:33,390
I mean, she has no prospects, no marketable skills.

105
00:04:33,390 --> 00:04:34,940
And then one day,

106
00:04:34,940 --> 00:04:37,220
she meets a group of geniuses

107
00:04:37,220 --> 00:04:39,320
and their friend Howard.

108
00:04:42,270 --> 00:04:42,850
Hey, I...

109
00:04:42,850 --> 00:04:44,790
Hang on. Let's see where he's going.

110
00:04:46,240 --> 00:04:48,860
She befriends them, and then lies in wait

111
00:04:48,860 --> 00:04:51,260
until they reveal a marketable idea,

112
00:04:51,260 --> 00:04:54,510
which she steals and sells to the highest bidder.

113
00:04:54,510 --> 00:04:56,650
- That is ridiculous. - Oh, is it?

114
00:04:56,650 --> 00:04:58,260
Let's see you come up with an explanation

115
00:04:58,260 --> 00:05:00,880
as to why this woman hangs out with us all the time.

116
00:05:05,040 --> 00:05:05,840
Oh, great. You know what?

117
00:05:05,840 --> 00:05:07,280
I've already mooched dinner off you guys.

118
00:05:07,280 --> 00:05:08,920
I don't need to listen to this.

119
00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:11,440
There's your answer-- free food.

120
00:05:12,570 --> 00:05:14,420
All right, I've taken the liberty

121
00:05:14,420 --> 00:05:16,050
of drafting these workflow charts

122
00:05:16,050 --> 00:05:19,150
which outline our various duties and the path we will follow

123
00:05:19,150 --> 00:05:21,560
as we develop our ground-breaking new app.

124
00:05:23,030 --> 00:05:25,550
Hey, why am I in charge of phone support?

125
00:05:28,120 --> 00:05:30,050
Seems a bit racist.

126
00:05:30,050 --> 00:05:31,830
A customer service representative

127
00:05:31,830 --> 00:05:34,190
with an Indian accent will create the impression

128
00:05:34,190 --> 00:05:37,890
we're a vast enterprise that uses overseas call centers.

129
00:05:37,890 --> 00:05:39,000
Oh.

130
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:40,040
Very clever.

131
00:05:40,040 --> 00:05:41,840
But still racist.

132
00:05:42,100 --> 00:05:45,220
Duly noted, Steve from Wichita.

133
00:05:46,650 --> 00:05:49,540
Why am I listed as your executive assistant?

134
00:05:49,540 --> 00:05:53,220
Because the word "Secretary" has fallen into disrepute.

135
00:05:53,650 --> 00:05:55,870
FYI, my mother's birthday is coming up.

136
00:05:55,870 --> 00:05:58,390
I'm going to need you to pick up a present.

137
00:05:59,150 --> 00:06:01,140
Okay, we need to stop for a minute.

138
00:06:01,140 --> 00:06:03,190
Leonard, please, we can talk during our break.

139
00:06:03,190 --> 00:06:04,460
We have one coming up at midnight.

140
00:06:04,460 --> 00:06:06,340
We'll need snacks.

141
00:06:06,340 --> 00:06:08,230
Sheldon, you're not in charge.

142
00:06:08,230 --> 00:06:09,740
It's my idea. I'm in charge.

143
00:06:09,740 --> 00:06:10,880
Oh, of course you are.

144
00:06:10,880 --> 00:06:12,250
Look at the organizational chart.

145
00:06:12,250 --> 00:06:14,300
You're clearly listed as founder.

146
00:06:14,300 --> 00:06:17,910
Well, yes, and you're listed as Chief Executive Officer,

147
00:06:17,910 --> 00:06:21,450
Chief Financial Officer and Chief Operating Officer.

148
00:06:21,450 --> 00:06:23,130
You missed Chief Science Officer,

149
00:06:23,130 --> 00:06:25,540
Chairman of the Board, and Head of the Secret Santa Committee.

150
00:06:25,540 --> 00:06:26,720
Okay.

151
00:06:27,940 --> 00:06:29,830
You need to get clear on this right now.

152
00:06:29,830 --> 00:06:32,700
I am in charge of this project.

153
00:06:33,190 --> 00:06:36,860
Ooh. Leonard's going all alpha nerd on Sheldon's ass...

154
00:06:37,940 --> 00:06:39,950
All right. All right, I'm not going to argue with you.

155
00:06:39,950 --> 00:06:42,400
Sheldon Cooper is nothing if not a team player.

156
00:06:42,400 --> 00:06:45,040
Dr. Hofstadter, you have the conn.

157
00:06:47,150 --> 00:06:48,310
Thank you.

158
00:06:49,410 --> 00:06:51,750
I thought we might start by talking about the user interface.

159
00:06:51,750 --> 00:06:53,560
Oh, dear Lord, we're doomed.

160
00:06:55,910 --> 00:06:57,760
So this button here

161
00:06:57,760 --> 00:06:59,850
will allow you to scan a new equation,

162
00:06:59,850 --> 00:07:01,530
but over here, you can press this,

163
00:07:01,530 --> 00:07:04,320
and just substitute new values for the coefficients.

164
00:07:04,320 --> 00:07:06,050
Good one, boss.

165
00:07:10,120 --> 00:07:11,000
What?

166
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:12,370
This diagram.

167
00:07:12,370 --> 00:07:14,920
I assume you were opening with a joke.

168
00:07:15,220 --> 00:07:18,440
It certainly buoyed up this employee's esprit de corps.

169
00:07:19,910 --> 00:07:22,170
It's not a joke. It's the real design.

170
00:07:22,600 --> 00:07:25,140
In that case, may I offer 27 little tweaks

171
00:07:25,140 --> 00:07:27,440
to make it slightly less embarrassing?

172
00:07:28,140 --> 00:07:29,540
Sheldon, I think this will work.

173
00:07:29,540 --> 00:07:31,200
Let's just try it my way.

174
00:07:31,200 --> 00:07:32,530
Oh, I'm-I'm sorry.

175
00:07:32,530 --> 00:07:33,520
I assumed that you wanted

176
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:35,550
candid truth-telling from your employees,

177
00:07:35,550 --> 00:07:38,120
but I realize now, you want the mindless nattering

178
00:07:38,120 --> 00:07:40,300
of complacent yes-men.

179
00:07:41,470 --> 00:07:42,210
Are you done?

180
00:07:42,210 --> 00:07:43,820
If you say so, boss.

181
00:07:46,420 --> 00:07:48,020
So right now, this button-press event

182
00:07:48,020 --> 00:07:49,980
is just triggering a dummy procedure call...

183
00:07:49,980 --> 00:07:52,400
How about this for the app name?

184
00:07:52,400 --> 00:07:55,000
The Surprisingly Helpful Equation-Linked

185
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:58,310
Differential Optimized Numerator.

186
00:07:59,190 --> 00:08:01,930
So it spells "Sheldon."

187
00:08:03,010 --> 00:08:04,090
Does it?

188
00:08:04,090 --> 00:08:06,150
A happy accident.

189
00:08:07,150 --> 00:08:10,070
We're not doing names now. Howard?

190
00:08:10,070 --> 00:08:12,470
Like I was saying, right now it's just a dummy procedure,

191
00:08:12,470 --> 00:08:13,920
but eventually, it will drop us into...

192
00:08:13,920 --> 00:08:15,840
All right, fine. If we're not going to give it a name,

193
00:08:15,840 --> 00:08:18,640
can we at least give it a secret code designation?

194
00:08:19,020 --> 00:08:20,360
- No. - Hear me out.

195
00:08:20,360 --> 00:08:22,630
The atomic bomb was The Manhattan Project.

196
00:08:22,630 --> 00:08:25,280
Windows 95 was originally Project Chicago.

197
00:08:25,280 --> 00:08:29,750
For our app, I would humbly suggest Project Nodlehs.

198
00:08:30,860 --> 00:08:33,260
That's just "Sheldon" Backwards.

199
00:08:33,760 --> 00:08:35,620
Another happy accident.

200
00:08:36,200 --> 00:08:39,190
We're not wasting time with names right now.

201
00:08:39,190 --> 00:08:40,540
I see.

202
00:08:41,170 --> 00:08:43,120
Is that decision open for discussion?

203
00:08:43,120 --> 00:08:44,460
No.

204
00:08:44,460 --> 00:08:46,590
Is the decision to not discuss it open for discussion?

205
00:08:46,590 --> 00:08:47,900
No.

206
00:08:48,470 --> 00:08:49,420
Again,

207
00:08:49,420 --> 00:08:51,760
right now it's just a dummy procedure,

208
00:08:51,760 --> 00:08:53,260
but eventually, it will drop us into...

209
00:08:53,260 --> 00:08:54,810
I call for a vote of no confidence

210
00:08:54,810 --> 00:08:56,510
and an immediate change in leadership.

211
00:08:56,510 --> 00:08:57,620
Sheldon, stop it.

212
00:08:57,620 --> 00:09:00,250
When in the course of human events, it becomes necessary

213
00:09:00,250 --> 00:09:03,080
to dissolve the bonds that tie three competent scientists together...

214
00:09:03,080 --> 00:09:05,070
- That's enough, Sheldon. - All right, let's get right to the vote.

215
00:09:05,070 --> 00:09:06,710
Show of hands-- all those opposed

216
00:09:06,710 --> 00:09:09,170
to Leonard and his reign of tyranny.

217
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:13,080
Okay, that's it, you're fired.

218
00:09:13,590 --> 00:09:15,120
Really?

219
00:09:15,640 --> 00:09:17,110
Why?

220
00:09:18,220 --> 00:09:21,390
Because you're impossible to work with.

221
00:09:21,390 --> 00:09:22,830
I see.

222
00:09:26,550 --> 00:09:28,210
And is that open for discussion?

223
00:09:34,340 --> 00:09:35,650
What are you doing?

224
00:09:35,650 --> 00:09:38,720
Oh. Good morning, shoemaker.

225
00:09:39,180 --> 00:09:40,200
I think you'll be pleased

226
00:09:40,200 --> 00:09:42,900
with what the elves were up to last night.

227
00:09:43,360 --> 00:09:44,500
I fired you.

228
00:09:44,500 --> 00:09:47,490
Oh, I know. I'm now an independent contractor.

229
00:09:48,260 --> 00:09:50,090
No, you are now nothing.

230
00:09:50,090 --> 00:09:53,460
You have no connection to this project whatsoever.

231
00:09:53,460 --> 00:09:55,450
But I made it better.

232
00:09:55,450 --> 00:09:58,400
I don't want it better. I want it my way.

233
00:09:58,400 --> 00:10:00,540
Well, that speaks volumes, doesn't it?

234
00:10:01,380 --> 00:10:05,580
All right, I suppose I'll go put on my bus pants.

235
00:10:05,580 --> 00:10:07,370
What the hell are bus pants?

236
00:10:07,370 --> 00:10:09,880
They are pants one wears over one's regular pants

237
00:10:09,880 --> 00:10:11,290
when one sits on bus seats

238
00:10:11,290 --> 00:10:13,570
that other people have previously sat on.

239
00:10:14,710 --> 00:10:16,930
But perhaps from your lofty heights

240
00:10:16,930 --> 00:10:19,060
atop the corporate ladder, you've lost touch

241
00:10:19,060 --> 00:10:21,760
with the struggles of the common man.

242
00:10:22,670 --> 00:10:25,290
Look, I'm still happy to drive you to work.

243
00:10:25,290 --> 00:10:26,750
Nothing's changed in that regard.

244
00:10:26,750 --> 00:10:29,310
We're still roommates, we're still friends.

245
00:10:29,310 --> 00:10:32,210
Except you identified me, your best friend,

246
00:10:32,210 --> 00:10:34,740
as a gangrenous limb that needed to be severed

247
00:10:34,740 --> 00:10:37,140
from the organism and tossed away.

248
00:10:37,140 --> 00:10:39,040
The desperate act of a little man

249
00:10:39,040 --> 00:10:41,560
with a big Napoleon complex.

250
00:10:43,790 --> 00:10:45,080
You know what?

251
00:10:45,080 --> 00:10:47,100
Go put on your bus pants.

252
00:10:52,990 --> 00:10:54,500
Mm. Last night, I had a dream

253
00:10:54,500 --> 00:10:56,440
we got so rich from the app,

254
00:10:56,440 --> 00:10:59,600
you and I bought matching side-by-side mansions.

255
00:11:00,270 --> 00:11:00,950
But...

256
00:11:00,950 --> 00:11:02,140
there was a secret tunnel

257
00:11:02,140 --> 00:11:05,750
connecting your front yard to my back yard.

258
00:11:10,340 --> 00:11:12,420
What do you think that means?

259
00:11:13,210 --> 00:11:16,970
It means after we play handball, I'm showering at home.

260
00:11:20,020 --> 00:11:22,110
Good morning, Friend Howard.

261
00:11:22,110 --> 00:11:23,380
Friend Raj.

262
00:11:23,380 --> 00:11:26,280
I see you gentlemen are enjoying beverages.

263
00:11:26,280 --> 00:11:30,100
Perhaps they would taste better out of these.

264
00:11:30,910 --> 00:11:32,540
"World's Greatest Astrophysicist"?

265
00:11:32,540 --> 00:11:35,350
Don't thank me. You earned it.

266
00:11:36,890 --> 00:11:39,260
"Howard Wolowitz."

267
00:11:41,440 --> 00:11:43,910
Why not "World's Greatest Engineer"?

268
00:11:43,910 --> 00:11:44,760
I'm sorry.

269
00:11:44,760 --> 00:11:46,630
I typed that into the label maker,

270
00:11:46,630 --> 00:11:48,880
but I just couldn't press enter.

271
00:11:48,880 --> 00:11:50,280
Now down to business.

272
00:11:50,280 --> 00:11:53,050
What would it take for you to abandon Leonard

273
00:11:53,050 --> 00:11:55,110
and join me in a rival company?

274
00:11:55,110 --> 00:11:57,180
Unless, of course, the mugs are sufficient,

275
00:11:57,180 --> 00:11:59,530
in which case, welcome aboard.

276
00:12:00,170 --> 00:12:01,720
We're not quitting on Leonard.

277
00:12:01,720 --> 00:12:04,730
I understand. Your loyalty is admirable.

278
00:12:04,730 --> 00:12:08,700
But what if I were to up the ante?

279
00:12:09,190 --> 00:12:10,820
What are you talking about?

280
00:12:10,820 --> 00:12:12,800
Monogrammed key chains

281
00:12:12,800 --> 00:12:15,070
with a built-in laser pointer.

282
00:12:19,160 --> 00:12:20,560
No?

283
00:12:21,410 --> 00:12:23,150
How about can cozies

284
00:12:23,150 --> 00:12:26,270
emblazoned with our university mascot?

285
00:12:27,590 --> 00:12:29,330
Go, Beavers.

286
00:12:31,950 --> 00:12:33,660
I'll be back.

287
00:12:35,440 --> 00:12:37,610
Okay, let's try this one.

288
00:12:40,110 --> 00:12:41,970
"Spherical Hankel function."

289
00:12:41,970 --> 00:12:43,480
Hold on.

290
00:12:43,760 --> 00:12:44,810
That's it!

291
00:12:44,810 --> 00:12:45,870
Eureka!

292
00:12:45,870 --> 00:12:47,440
Hey, we agreed when it was "Eureka" Time,

293
00:12:47,440 --> 00:12:49,630
we were all gonna say it together.

294
00:12:50,290 --> 00:12:51,780
Fine. Let's say it together.

295
00:12:51,780 --> 00:12:53,850
No. The moment has passed.

296
00:12:54,950 --> 00:12:56,960
Greetings, gentlemen.

297
00:12:56,960 --> 00:12:58,740
How goes your little project?

298
00:12:58,740 --> 00:13:01,380
You mean, "Project Lenwoloppali"?

299
00:13:02,170 --> 00:13:05,120
Ah, a combination of all your names.

300
00:13:05,120 --> 00:13:06,330
Very clever.

301
00:13:06,330 --> 00:13:09,660
I assume "Koothranardowitz" Was taken?

302
00:13:10,970 --> 00:13:13,420
We're actually scanning equations and getting good results.

303
00:13:13,420 --> 00:13:15,920
Oh, well then, by all means, carry on.

304
00:13:15,920 --> 00:13:17,930
I wouldn't want to impede your progress.

305
00:13:17,930 --> 00:13:20,380
Howard, did you solve the install time problem yet?

306
00:13:20,380 --> 00:13:22,010
No. It's a little tricky.

307
00:13:22,010 --> 00:13:24,290
I'm gonna try having it pick up the libraries dyn...

308
00:13:31,750 --> 00:13:33,420
What are you doing?!

309
00:13:34,250 --> 00:13:36,040
Playing the theremin.

310
00:13:38,270 --> 00:13:41,580
No. I mean, what are you doing with a theremin?

311
00:13:42,380 --> 00:13:44,030
Playing it.

312
00:13:45,180 --> 00:13:47,270
I've loved the theremin from the first moment

313
00:13:47,270 --> 00:13:49,350
I heard the original Star Trek theme.

314
00:13:49,350 --> 00:13:50,680
And it's been killing me

315
00:13:50,680 --> 00:13:53,780
that it just sits in my closet, gathering dust.

316
00:14:08,170 --> 00:14:10,600
Sheldon! We're working here!

317
00:14:10,600 --> 00:14:11,620
That's all right.

318
00:14:11,620 --> 00:14:13,770
I can barely hear you over my theremin.

319
00:14:21,570 --> 00:14:23,660
Well, that was a little uncalled for.

320
00:14:23,660 --> 00:14:27,000
No, that was completely called for. We need quiet.

321
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:29,140
So, your project is more important than mine?

322
00:14:29,140 --> 00:14:33,300
Well, seeing as your project is to sabotage my project, yes.

323
00:14:33,300 --> 00:14:34,960
Don't beat around the bush, Leonard.

324
00:14:34,960 --> 00:14:35,880
If you don't want me here,

325
00:14:35,880 --> 00:14:37,960
just say the word, and I'll leave.

326
00:14:42,890 --> 00:14:45,190
Could have beaten around the bush a little.

327
00:14:49,580 --> 00:14:53,210
Nobody knows

328
00:14:53,210 --> 00:14:56,000
The trouble I've seen

329
00:14:56,710 --> 00:15:02,100
Nobody knows my sorrow ?

330
00:15:06,040 --> 00:15:07,100
What you doing?

331
00:15:07,100 --> 00:15:09,370
Trying to contact your home planet?

332
00:15:10,260 --> 00:15:12,240
I'm practicing my theremin.

333
00:15:13,330 --> 00:15:16,250
Nobody knows

334
00:15:16,250 --> 00:15:19,100
The trouble I've seen

335
00:15:19,100 --> 00:15:23,430
Glory, hallelujah.

336
00:15:24,850 --> 00:15:27,160
Something got you down, there, Sheldon?

337
00:15:27,580 --> 00:15:29,920
Leonard kicked me out of my own apartment.

338
00:15:29,920 --> 00:15:31,610
And his app team.

339
00:15:31,610 --> 00:15:33,280
Oh, honey, I'm sorry.

340
00:15:33,280 --> 00:15:34,910
He wouldn't drive me to work today.

341
00:15:34,910 --> 00:15:36,810
I had to wear my bus pants.

342
00:15:37,710 --> 00:15:39,230
- Is that so? - Yes.

343
00:15:39,230 --> 00:15:40,510
And they're still sticky.

344
00:15:41,810 --> 00:15:43,790
Probably going to have to throw them away.

345
00:15:46,160 --> 00:15:48,420
Hey, you don't need Leonard and his app.

346
00:15:48,420 --> 00:15:50,110
You can make one with me.

347
00:15:50,110 --> 00:15:51,770
With you?

348
00:15:53,440 --> 00:15:55,450
Seriously, I have a great idea for one.

349
00:15:55,450 --> 00:15:57,850
Is it better than your idea to move to Los Angeles

350
00:15:57,850 --> 00:15:59,730
and become a famous actress?

351
00:16:01,880 --> 00:16:02,430
Okay, look...

352
00:16:02,430 --> 00:16:04,110
when you see someone wearing shoes you like,

353
00:16:04,110 --> 00:16:05,730
you just snap a picture of them,

354
00:16:05,730 --> 00:16:07,130
and the app goes on the Internet

355
00:16:07,130 --> 00:16:09,530
to find out where you can buy them.

356
00:16:09,530 --> 00:16:11,040
That's your app idea?

357
00:16:11,040 --> 00:16:11,940
Well, you don't like it?

358
00:16:11,940 --> 00:16:14,800
I didn't say that. But no, I don't.

359
00:16:15,670 --> 00:16:16,550
Why not?

360
00:16:16,550 --> 00:16:18,290
Oh, Penny, where do I begin?

361
00:16:18,290 --> 00:16:20,450
The simple-mindedness of your idea

362
00:16:20,450 --> 00:16:24,100
is exceeded only by its crass consumerism and banality.

363
00:16:24,720 --> 00:16:25,890
And Leonard didn't want to work with you?

364
00:16:25,890 --> 00:16:27,580
Imagine that.

365
00:16:27,580 --> 00:16:30,110
I know, it's baffling.

366
00:16:31,080 --> 00:16:34,700
Nobody knows

367
00:16:34,700 --> 00:16:37,490
The trouble I've seen...

368
00:16:38,170 --> 00:16:39,550
Sheldon, I'm gonna make some cocoa.

369
00:16:39,550 --> 00:16:40,850
Do you want some?

370
00:16:41,490 --> 00:16:43,010
Do you make it with milk or water?

371
00:16:43,010 --> 00:16:44,690
- Milk. - Real cocoa?

372
00:16:44,690 --> 00:16:46,540
That's what it says on the packet.

373
00:16:47,430 --> 00:16:48,830
Do you have those little marshmallows?

374
00:16:48,830 --> 00:16:50,700
No, sorry.

375
00:16:50,700 --> 00:16:52,660
Well, I suppose it's appropriate.

376
00:16:52,660 --> 00:16:53,450
What does that mean?

377
00:16:53,450 --> 00:16:56,630
A disappointing drink for a disappointing day.

378
00:17:00,150 --> 00:17:01,140
Hey, look at this.

379
00:17:01,140 --> 00:17:03,110
20 people from the university have already signed up

380
00:17:03,110 --> 00:17:04,710
for our private beta.

381
00:17:04,710 --> 00:17:05,990
I'm telling you,

382
00:17:05,990 --> 00:17:08,100
the Lenwoloppali Differential Equation Scanner

383
00:17:08,100 --> 00:17:10,800
meets a real need. We've got a hit.

384
00:17:11,330 --> 00:17:12,700
What do you think we should sell it for?

385
00:17:12,700 --> 00:17:14,890
Well, based on the fact that our potential market

386
00:17:14,890 --> 00:17:16,890
is about 70 or 80 guys,

387
00:17:16,890 --> 00:17:17,960
and you want a submarine,

388
00:17:17,960 --> 00:17:20,210
I'd say about a million dollars a pop.

389
00:17:21,150 --> 00:17:23,430
Well, what if we put out a lite version for half a million?

390
00:17:23,430 --> 00:17:25,370
You know, get the word of mouth going.

391
00:17:26,790 --> 00:17:28,180
Hi. I'm sorry to bother you guys,

392
00:17:28,180 --> 00:17:30,760
but you've got to come take your Sheldon back.

393
00:17:30,760 --> 00:17:32,010
What's he doing in your apartment?

394
00:17:32,010 --> 00:17:34,550
Well, he was moping down in the lobby, so I invited him over.

395
00:17:34,550 --> 00:17:35,730
Now I regret it.

396
00:17:35,730 --> 00:17:38,460
Why do you regret... Uh, never mind, stupid question.

397
00:17:38,460 --> 00:17:41,290
Look, can't you just let him play with you until bedtime?

398
00:17:42,750 --> 00:17:43,810
We're not playing.

399
00:17:43,810 --> 00:17:45,050
This is real work.

400
00:17:45,050 --> 00:17:46,970
We're going to be hundred-aires.

401
00:17:47,870 --> 00:17:52,080
Okay. What if I got him to apologize and promise to behave?

402
00:17:54,510 --> 00:17:57,060
Then I guess we would let him back on the app team,

403
00:17:57,060 --> 00:17:59,470
and while we're doing that, you could take an aerial tour

404
00:17:59,470 --> 00:18:02,170
of L.A. on your flying pig.

405
00:18:03,250 --> 00:18:04,720
Okay. Wait right here.

406
00:18:06,620 --> 00:18:08,890
I'll tell you one thing. If I get rich enough,

407
00:18:08,890 --> 00:18:09,960
that's the kind of girl I want to take

408
00:18:09,960 --> 00:18:11,900
on a submarine ride.

409
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:15,750
And yes, that time, I meant it to be dirty.

410
00:18:18,490 --> 00:18:19,820
Really?

411
00:18:19,820 --> 00:18:21,290
Leonard wants me back?

412
00:18:21,290 --> 00:18:24,940
Oh, yes. He says they are lost without you.

413
00:18:25,670 --> 00:18:27,890
Well, we sure saw that coming.

414
00:18:28,620 --> 00:18:29,520
That's right.

415
00:18:29,520 --> 00:18:30,880
You know what I'm going to do?

416
00:18:30,880 --> 00:18:34,990
I am going to sit here and just let them stew.

417
00:18:38,330 --> 00:18:39,310
No, no, no. You know,

418
00:18:39,310 --> 00:18:40,750
they have suffered enough.

419
00:18:40,750 --> 00:18:43,600
Everybody's suffered enough.

420
00:18:44,160 --> 00:18:46,390
The thing is, you're going to have to offer him

421
00:18:46,390 --> 00:18:49,140
a face-saving way out of this.

422
00:18:49,140 --> 00:18:50,090
How?

423
00:18:50,090 --> 00:18:51,180
Say you're sorry.

424
00:18:51,180 --> 00:18:52,380
Oh, no.

425
00:18:52,380 --> 00:18:55,670
Mrs. Mary Cooper didn't raise her no liars.

426
00:18:57,310 --> 00:18:59,460
Okay. Um...

427
00:18:59,460 --> 00:19:00,990
How about this? You know how you're always trying

428
00:19:00,990 --> 00:19:02,530
to learn about sarcasm?

429
00:19:02,530 --> 00:19:04,240
- No.  - No?

430
00:19:04,240 --> 00:19:06,200
I was being sarcastic.

431
00:19:07,030 --> 00:19:08,750
Oh. Good for you.

432
00:19:08,750 --> 00:19:10,140
So all you have to do here is say

433
00:19:10,140 --> 00:19:11,290
you're sorry to Leonard,

434
00:19:11,290 --> 00:19:13,520
but say it sarcastically.

435
00:19:13,520 --> 00:19:15,240
Of course.

436
00:19:15,650 --> 00:19:18,290
He will hear it as an attempt to mend fences,

437
00:19:18,290 --> 00:19:20,420
as opposed to the withering condemnation

438
00:19:20,420 --> 00:19:22,550
you and I will know it to be.

439
00:19:23,330 --> 00:19:24,770
Yeah. Yeah, that was my plan.

440
00:19:24,770 --> 00:19:25,980
All right. Come on. Let's go.

441
00:19:25,980 --> 00:19:27,770
Oh, by the way, thank you

442
00:19:27,770 --> 00:19:29,490
for the delicious cocoa.

443
00:19:29,490 --> 00:19:31,140
Oh, you're welcome.

444
00:19:31,140 --> 00:19:33,370
Boy, I'm getting good at this.

445
00:19:35,790 --> 00:19:37,680
Okay, now that you're back on the team,

446
00:19:37,680 --> 00:19:39,080
let's get you caught up.

447
00:19:39,080 --> 00:19:41,330
Before you begin, let me say again

448
00:19:41,330 --> 00:19:44,630
how deeply sorry I am for my earlier behavior

449
00:19:44,630 --> 00:19:49,020
and how much I respect and admire your leadership.

450
00:19:50,390 --> 00:19:52,030
Thank you.

451
00:19:56,290 --> 00:19:57,970
So as you can see,

452
00:19:57,970 --> 00:20:00,760
not only can you store your favorite equations,

453
00:20:00,760 --> 00:20:03,040
but you can forward them to your friends

454
00:20:03,040 --> 00:20:06,650
or post them on Facebook right from the app.

455
00:20:06,650 --> 00:20:08,850
Well, I must say, I am impressed.

456
00:20:08,850 --> 00:20:09,530
Thanks.

457
00:20:09,530 --> 00:20:12,050
You all have clearly put in a lot of hard work.

458
00:20:12,050 --> 00:20:12,930
We have.

459
00:20:12,930 --> 00:20:13,840
As a result, you have taken

460
00:20:13,840 --> 00:20:16,480
the most important step on the road to success:

461
00:20:16,480 --> 00:20:17,860
learning what not to do.

462
00:20:17,860 --> 00:20:19,660
Now, let's start fresh.

463
00:20:20,760 --> 00:20:22,770
Howard, we're going to need some tea.

464
00:20:29,030 --> 00:20:32,140
Okay, these are Uggs.

465
00:20:32,140 --> 00:20:34,950
These are Crocs.

466
00:20:34,950 --> 00:20:36,930
These are knockoff Manolo Blahniks.

467
00:20:36,930 --> 00:20:38,440
Bored.

468
00:20:39,130 --> 00:20:41,300
Look, you said it yourself, we have to create a database

469
00:20:41,300 --> 00:20:43,460
before you can write an alga-thingy.

470
00:20:43,460 --> 00:20:44,960
Algorithm.

471
00:20:44,960 --> 00:20:47,420
You see, Penny, Alan Turing defined an algorithm...

472
00:20:47,420 --> 00:20:48,910
Bored.

473
00:20:49,480 --> 00:20:50,050
Okay.

474
00:20:50,050 --> 00:20:51,850
These are Steve Maddens.

475
00:20:51,850 --> 00:20:53,770
These are Nine West.

476
00:20:53,770 --> 00:20:56,800
These are Target. Oh, but don't they look like Chanel?

477
00:20:57,770 --> 00:20:58,630
These are Michael Kors...

478
00:20:58,630 --> 00:21:00,790
Nobody knows...

479
00:21:00,790 --> 00:21:02,860
the trouble I've seen

480
00:21:02,860 --> 00:21:04,880
Nobody knows...

481
00:21:04,880 --> 00:21:06,010
my sorrows

